====================== BONUS3 WALKTHROUGH =======================

=> the program allocates 132 bytes for buffer and opens the .pass file in read mode (r), then initialize each character with 0 using memset.

=> the program checks if (ac != 2) it returns immediately so we should add exactly one argument.

=> inside the else statement, fread() reads 66 character from the password file and put them inside buffer then 0.

=> afterward, buffer[atoi(av[1])] = 0; we can inject a value with av[1] argument and we know that a non-integer input will cause atoi() to return 0.

=> fread(&buffer[66], 1, 65, f); the program start reading again but f is already pointing at the EOF since it copied the whole password already.

=======================================
    if (strcmp(buffer, av[1]) == 0)
        execl("/bin/sh", "sh", NULL);
=======================================

==> this if statement is the most important part, we need that the buffer to be equal to av[1] so the strcmp returns 0 so the shell opens for us.

a. (buffer[0] = 0) => buffer here will be concidered as an empty string.
b. so if we pass an empty string in our arguement atoi will convert it as 0 so the buffer becomes empty, so strcmp(buffer, av[1]) will return 0,
and then we got our shell:

